WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.750
- I'm Dr. Sam Rader.

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.961
I'm a licensed clinical psychologist,

00:00:03.961 --> 00:00:05.555
and I'm going to be answering some of your

00:00:05.555 --> 00:00:07.064
mental health questions.

00:00:07.064 --> 00:00:09.814
(pleasant music)

00:00:19.257 --> 00:00:21.603
In my understanding,
personality isn't something

00:00:21.603 --> 00:00:22.780
you're necessarily born with.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.370
It's something that gets shaped
through early experience.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.336
We learn what parts of us feel acceptable

00:00:27.336 --> 00:00:29.260
in our family system,
what parts of us feel

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:33.102
unacceptable, and we adjust
all the parts of ourselves

00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:36.079
to shape into a personality
that's gonna fit

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:37.297
in our family system.

00:00:37.297 --> 00:00:40.518
And what I do as clinical psychologist, is

00:00:40.518 --> 00:00:42.859
I help my clients look
at their personality.

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:44.766
For example, if someone's really shy,

00:00:44.766 --> 00:00:47.032
we try to understand
what that's really about,

00:00:47.032 --> 00:00:48.839
and see if we can find more confidence.

00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:51.589
(pleasant music)

00:00:57.335 --> 00:01:00.482
Clinically speaking,
something becomes a disorder

00:01:00.482 --> 00:01:02.657
when it starts really getting in the way

00:01:02.657 --> 00:01:05.292
of you being able to function as a person

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:06.638
in relationships, at work.

00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:08.995
Then yeah, it might be some
kind of anxiety disorder,

00:01:08.995 --> 00:01:10.524
and you might wanna seek treatment.

00:01:10.524 --> 00:01:14.066
Otherwise, if you're able to
have a fairly normal life,

00:01:14.066 --> 00:01:16.209
but big waves of anxiety
come now and then.

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:19.069
All I can say is, join the club.

00:01:19.069 --> 00:01:20.713
It's normal to have some anxiety sometime.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.110
It's really great though,
to have some practices

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.407
to know how to self soothe
when you have that anxiety.

00:01:25.407 --> 00:01:27.990
(upbeat music)

00:01:28.972 --> 00:01:30.603
I'm just gonna go with, no.

00:01:30.603 --> 00:01:33.474
How 'bout not talking about
my friends with my friends?

00:01:33.474 --> 00:01:35.212
You know how much better
if feels to not gossip?

00:01:35.212 --> 00:01:38.120
So yes, the sacredness of
protecting the confidentiality

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.546
of my clients comes first.

00:01:39.546 --> 00:01:41.930
And I'm learning from that
how sacred it might be

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.328
in my life outside of here to keep

00:01:43.328 --> 00:01:45.282
confidence in friendships.

00:01:45.282 --> 00:01:48.032
(pleasant music)

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.575
When tears just spill out for seemingly

00:01:54.575 --> 00:01:56.585
no apparent reason, my best guess at why

00:01:56.585 --> 00:01:58.539
that's happening is that maybe feelings

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:00.860
have been held inside too often.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.011
When we go for days or weeks or months

00:02:03.011 --> 00:02:05.482
with not expressing
ourselves, not reaching out

00:02:05.482 --> 00:02:07.633
when we need help, not
getting enough hugs,

00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:10.942
sometimes we can fill up like a bucket,

00:02:10.942 --> 00:02:14.010
and when that emotion bucket
gets all the way to the top,

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.412
the water is going to spill out.

00:02:15.412 --> 00:02:18.122
And that's gonna spill out in
the form of tears sometimes,

00:02:18.122 --> 00:02:20.104
even when it makes no
sense why we're crying.

00:02:20.104 --> 00:02:22.854
(pleasant music)

00:02:25.682 --> 00:02:28.777
There's so much less of a
stigma around depression now.

00:02:28.777 --> 00:02:31.147
It's probably safe to tell most employers

00:02:31.147 --> 00:02:32.461
if you're suffering with it.

00:02:32.461 --> 00:02:33.990
Especially if that will
help them understand

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.421
if things are changing a
little bit in your work style.

00:02:36.421 --> 00:02:38.487
There's no shame in
asking for what you need,

00:02:38.487 --> 00:02:40.833
and ultimately your
employer, if they're someone

00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:43.632
who's a good business
person, will be really happy

00:02:43.632 --> 00:02:46.109
that they know what's
going on, because then they

00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:49.696
can help support you in
continuing to do good work.

00:02:49.696 --> 00:02:52.279
(upbeat music)

00:02:57.546 --> 00:03:00.340
Well, whoever you are,
I adore you, and I agree

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.173
with you completely.

00:03:01.173 --> 00:03:02.484
Touch is so healing.

00:03:02.484 --> 00:03:03.865
I have a lot of answers to this question.

00:03:03.865 --> 00:03:07.627
The first is that maybe even
massage is enough for you,

00:03:07.627 --> 00:03:09.802
who really knows how to
hit all those sweet spots.

00:03:09.802 --> 00:03:12.369
In terms of traditional
therapy, it's often frowned upon

00:03:12.369 --> 00:03:13.666
to touch your clients.

00:03:13.666 --> 00:03:16.488
There is a style of therapy
that's really wonderful,

00:03:16.488 --> 00:03:18.588
called Somatic Experiencing.

00:03:18.588 --> 00:03:21.160
In one of the types of
Somatic Experiencing,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.368
touch is used as part of the therapy.

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:25.644
So Somatic Experiencing
is basically sitting,

00:03:25.644 --> 00:03:29.146
sensing into your body,
noticing what you feel where,

00:03:29.146 --> 00:03:32.106
and allowing the body to heal
itself; whether it's trauma,

00:03:32.106 --> 00:03:33.948
or pain, emotionally or physically.

00:03:33.948 --> 00:03:36.698
(pleasant music)

00:03:38.653 --> 00:03:40.423
That question has a lot
going on inside of it.

00:03:40.423 --> 00:03:42.999
I have taken many, many
clients who swear up and down

00:03:42.999 --> 00:03:44.713
they have incurable depression, and

00:03:44.713 --> 00:03:45.953
we get them feeling better.

00:03:45.953 --> 00:03:48.424
If there's a way to latch back on to life,

00:03:48.424 --> 00:03:50.321
there's a way to come out of depression.

00:03:50.321 --> 00:03:53.587
But if you have a stubborn
depression, I encourage you

00:03:53.587 --> 00:03:55.507
to keep fighting for wellness.

00:03:55.507 --> 00:03:57.097
We've all been in those dark moments.

00:03:57.097 --> 00:04:01.445
It's just a state of mind
because you are loved,

00:04:01.445 --> 00:04:03.999
you are cared about, and
that somebody wants to hear

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:07.079
from you today if you're
struggling with suicidal thoughts.

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:09.829
(pleasant music)

00:04:12.336 --> 00:04:14.818
What people imagine is going on in therapy

00:04:14.818 --> 00:04:16.505
is that it's gotta be very clinical.

00:04:16.505 --> 00:04:18.821
They're my doctor, and I have to sort of,

00:04:18.821 --> 00:04:21.077
not tell them what I'm thinking
and feeling about them.

00:04:21.077 --> 00:04:23.388
Most of the work that I do with my clients

00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:26.391
is in the transference,
which is the relationship

00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:28.068
between myself and the client.

00:04:28.068 --> 00:04:30.547
So if a client came in
and said, hey, this isn't

00:04:30.547 --> 00:04:32.976
working for me, I don't like
the way you're doing this,

00:04:32.976 --> 00:04:34.539
I would think, Yeah!

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:36.530
Jackpot, we're really gonna get somewhere.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.740
Because if we're able to
talk about our relationship,

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:42.035
what's going on between us,
how it feels, what they like

00:04:42.035 --> 00:04:43.698
about what I'm doing,
what they don't like about

00:04:43.698 --> 00:04:44.531
what I'm doing.

00:04:44.531 --> 00:04:47.403
This might be the beginning
of a really exciting time

00:04:47.403 --> 00:04:48.840
of growth with your therapist.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.689
So if all else fails,
and you're really sure

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:53.128
it's not a match for you,
you can just let them know,

00:04:53.128 --> 00:04:55.872
and say, thanks for our time
together, I'm clear that

00:04:55.872 --> 00:04:57.394
you and I are not a match.

00:04:57.394 --> 00:04:58.619
I wish you all the best and this will be

00:04:58.619 --> 00:05:00.004
my last session today.

00:05:00.004 --> 00:05:02.419
I think it's so cool
that we live in a time

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:04.903
where people are becoming
more and more aware

00:05:04.903 --> 00:05:06.100
of mental health.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.900
There are countless self
help books out there,

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.772
resources on the internet,
even apps that can help you

00:05:11.772 --> 00:05:12.765
with depression.

00:05:12.765 --> 00:05:14.928
Find ways to get back out into your life.

00:05:14.928 --> 00:05:17.080
Have real genuine experiences.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.439
Share your feelings.

00:05:18.439 --> 00:05:20.904
Laugh, dance, sing, cry.

00:05:20.904 --> 00:05:21.931
This is life.

00:05:21.931 --> 00:05:24.638
A lot of clients ask me if
it's possible to change.

00:05:24.638 --> 00:05:27.743
And in my experience the
answer is absolutely, yes.

00:05:27.743 --> 00:05:28.736
(signal ding)

00:05:28.736 --> 00:05:29.821
(laughs)

00:05:29.821 --> 00:05:30.707
Ding!

00:05:30.707 --> 00:05:33.290
(upbeat music)

